  "contracts": {
    "thm-local-leading-eigenvector-stability-under-eigengap": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "cor-real-spectral-theorem-for-self-adjoint-endomorphisms",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $V$ be a finite-dimensional real inner product space and let $T:V\\to V$ be\nself-adjoint. Then $V$ has an orthonormal basis consisting of eigenvectors of\n$T$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-courant-fischer-min-max-principle",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $T:V\\to V$ be self-adjoint on an $n$-dimensional real inner product space,\nand list its eigenvalues in weakly decreasing order:\n\n$$\\lambda_1\\ge\\lambda_2\\ge\\cdots\\ge\\lambda_n.$$\n\nThen for every $1\\le k\\le n$,\n\n$$\\lambda_k=\\min_{\\dim W=n-k+1}\\ \\max_{0\\ne v\\in W} R_T(v)=\\max_{\\dim U=k}\\ \\min_{0\\ne v\\in U} R_T(v).$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-operator-norm-on-a-finite-dimensional-inner-product-space",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $T:V\\to W$ be a linear map between finite-dimensional real or complex inner\nproduct spaces. If $V=0$, define $\\lVert T\\rVert:=0$. If $V\\ne0$, define the\n**operator norm** of $T$ by\n\n$$\\lVert T\\rVert:=\\max\\{\\lVert Tv\\rVert:\\lVert v\\rVert=1\\}.$$\n\nThe next theorem proves that this maximum exists and equals the largest\nsingular value.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "def-euclidean-inner-product",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$. A natural number is a von Neumann natural, that is a set,\nand $n = \\{0, 1, \\dots, n-1\\}$ ([[def-natural-numbers]],\n[[lem-nat-order-is-membership]]), so\n\n$$\\mathbb{R}^{n} \\;=\\; \\{\\, x \\;:\\; x \\text{ is a function } n \\to \\mathbb{R} \\,\\}$$\n\nis the function space of [[def-function-space]] at $F = \\mathbb{R}$ and $X = n$,\na vector space over $\\mathbb{R}$ under the pointwise operations\n([[def-vector-space]]). We write $x_k := x(k)$ for $k < n$, and two elements of\n$\\mathbb{R}^{n}$ are equal exactly when they agree at every $k < n$. This is the\nsame set that [[lem-metrics-on-rn]] calls $\\mathbb{R}^{n}$.\n\nThe **Euclidean inner product** of $x, y \\in \\mathbb{R}^{n}$ is the real number\n\n$$\\langle x, y\\rangle \\;:=\\; \\sum_{k<n} x_k\\,y_k ,$$\n\nthe finite sum of [[def-finite-sum]] applied to the list $k \\mapsto x_k y_k$\n(extended by $0$ beyond $n$, as every finite list in this library is). The\n**Euclidean norm** of $x$ is\n\n$$\\lVert x\\rVert_2 \\;:=\\; \\sqrt{\\langle x, x\\rangle},$$\n\nwhich is defined because $\\langle x,x\\rangle = \\sum_{k<n} x_k^{2} \\ge 0$ (a sum\nof nonnegative terms, [[lem-finite-sum-laws]] clause 4 and\n[[lem-of-square-positive]], the case $x_k = 0$ giving $x_k^2 = 0$ by\n[[def-integer-power]]) and every nonnegative real has a unique nonnegative square\nroot ([[thm-of-square-roots]]).\n\n### Both are defined for every $n$, including $n = 0$\n\nAt $n = 0$ the set $\\mathbb{R}^{0}$ has exactly one element, the empty function,\nand it is the zero vector space ([[lem-standard-basis-of-f-n]] clause 5); the sum\nabove is the empty sum, so $\\langle x,y\\rangle = 0$ and $\\lVert x\\rVert_2 = 0$.\n**This is the first place on this page where the two index regimes diverge, and\nthe divergence is deliberate.** The published metrics $d_1$, $d_2$, $d_\\infty$ of\n[[lem-metrics-on-rn]] are defined only for $n \\ge 1$, because $d_\\infty$ would\notherwise be a maximum over the empty index set; the algebra above needs no such\nrestriction. The boundary in this page runs between the algebra and the metric,\nnot where a reader would guess, and [[rem-rn-conventions-and-scope]] lists\nexactly which items inherit $n \\ge 1$.\n\n### The algebra of the inner product\n\nFor all $x, y, z \\in \\mathbb{R}^{n}$ and $\\lambda \\in \\mathbb{R}$:\n\n1. **Symmetry.** $\\langle x,y\\rangle = \\langle y,x\\rangle$, since $x_ky_k = y_kx_k$\n   termwise.\n2. **Additivity in the first argument.**\n   $\\langle x + y, z\\rangle = \\langle x,z\\rangle + \\langle y,z\\rangle$: the list\n   $k \\mapsto (x_k+y_k)z_k$ is the termwise sum of $k \\mapsto x_kz_k$ and\n   $k \\mapsto y_kz_k$, so [[lem-finite-sum-laws]] clause 1 applies.\n3. **Homogeneity in the first argument.**\n   $\\langle \\lambda x, y\\rangle = \\lambda\\langle x,y\\rangle$, by\n   [[lem-finite-sum-laws]] clause 2.\n4. **Bilinearity.** Clauses 2 and 3 together with symmetry give the same two laws\n   in the second argument.\n5. **Positive definiteness.** $\\langle x,x\\rangle \\ge 0$, and\n   $\\langle x,x\\rangle = 0$ if and only if $x = 0$. Indeed a vanishing sum of\n   nonnegative terms has every term $0$ ([[lem-finite-sum-laws]] clause 4), so\n   $x_k^{2} = 0$ for every $k < n$, and a nonzero real has a positive square\n   ([[lem-of-square-positive]]), whence $x_k = 0$ for every $k < n$ and $x = 0$.\n6. **Agreement with the published Euclidean metric.** For $n \\ge 1$ and\n   $x, y \\in \\mathbb{R}^{n}$,\n   $\\lVert x - y\\rVert_2 = \\sqrt{\\sum_{k<n}(x_k-y_k)^{2}} = d_2(x,y)$, the two\n   sides being the same expression ([[lem-metrics-on-rn]]). In particular\n   $\\lVert x\\rVert_2 = d_2(x, 0)$.\n\nThat $\\lVert\\cdot\\rVert_2$ is a norm in the sense of [[def-norm-and-normed-space]]\nis proved in [[thm-cauchy-schwarz-and-the-euclidean-norm]], where the triangle\ninequality is obtained from the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality; it is not assumed\nhere.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-cauchy-schwarz-in-an-inner-product-space",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For all vectors $x,y$ in a real or complex inner-product space,\n\n$$|\\langle x,y\\rangle|\\le\\|x\\|\\,\\|y\\| ,$$\n\nwith equality if and only if $x$ and $y$ are linearly dependent.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "For every unit vector $x$, [F3] and [F4] give $|x^{\\mathsf T}Ex|\\le\\|x\\|_2\\|Ex\\|_2\\le\\epsilon$. The top-eigenvalue Rayleigh characterization in [F2] therefore gives $\\lambda_1(A+E)\\le\\lambda_1(A)+\\epsilon$, while evaluating the perturbed Rayleigh quotient at $v$ gives $\\lambda_1(A+E)\\ge v^{\\mathsf T}(A+E)v\\ge\\lambda_1(A)-\\epsilon$. This proves the eigenvalue bound.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "The minimum--maximum formula in [F2], with the $(p-1)$-dimensional subspace $v^\\perp$, gives $\\lambda_2(A+E)\\le\\max_{\\|x\\|_2=1,\\ x\\perp v}x^{\\mathsf T}(A+E)x \\le\\lambda_2(A)+\\epsilon$, since every such $x$ has $x^{\\mathsf T}Ax\\le\\lambda_2(A)$. Step 1.1 gives $\\lambda_1(A+E)\\ge\\lambda_1(A)-\\epsilon$, hence $\\lambda_1(A+E)-\\lambda_2(A+E)\\ge g-2\\epsilon>0$; the perturbed leading eigenvalue is simple. Also $\\lambda_1(A+E)-\\lambda_2(A)\\ge g-\\epsilon>0$.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "Let $P=I-vv^{\\mathsf T}$, the orthogonal projection onto $v^\\perp$, and write $\\widetilde\\lambda_1=\\lambda_1(A+E)$. Projecting $(A+E)\\widetilde v=\\widetilde\\lambda_1\\widetilde v$ onto $v^\\perp$ gives $(\\widetilde\\lambda_1 I-A|_{v^\\perp})P\\widetilde v=PE\\widetilde v$. In an orthonormal eigenbasis of $A|_{v^\\perp}$, every eigenvalue of $\\widetilde\\lambda_1 I-A|_{v^\\perp}$ is at least $\\widetilde\\lambda_1-\\lambda_2(A)\\ge g-\\epsilon$ by step 2.1. Therefore $(g-\\epsilon)\\|P\\widetilde v\\|_2\\le\\|PE\\widetilde v\\|_2\\le\\epsilon$. Indeed, $z=Pz+(I-P)z$ is an orthogonal decomposition, so $\\|Pz\\|_2\\le\\|z\\|_2$; then [F3] and $\\|\\widetilde v\\|_2=1$ give the final inequality. Since $\\|P\\widetilde v\\|_2^2=1-(v^{\\mathsf T}\\widetilde v)^2$, division by $g-\\epsilon>0$ proves the angle bound.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F3",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The dimension is p>=2; zero-dimensional matrices are excluded."
        },
        {
          "case": "zero",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Epsilon=0 is permitted and step 3.1 gives zero angle; step 1.1 gives zero eigenvalue change."
        },
        {
          "case": "one",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The condition g=lambda_1-lambda_2 requires p>=2."
        },
        {
          "case": "degenerate",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "The original top eigenvalue is simple and epsilon<g/2 strictly; step 2.1 proves the perturbed gap is positive."
        },
        {
          "case": "endpoints",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "The strict endpoint epsilon=g/2 is excluded by the statement; step 2.1 uses the strict inequality."
        },
        {
          "case": "nonempty-choice",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "The statement accepts any supplied unit eigenvectors; step 3.1 uses the real spectral theorem without a choice axiom."
        },
        {
          "case": "iff-forward",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "For a simple top eigenvalue and ||E||op<g/2, Proof2.1 preserves the gap and3.1 bounds the line angle by epsilon/(g-epsilon). These are sufficient local estimates, not a characterization of perturbations for which the leading line stays simple or stable. The forward boundary would require a separate asserted biconditional, which this specific claim does not contain."
        },
        {
          "case": "iff-reverse",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "For a simple top eigenvalue and ||E||op<g/2, Proof2.1 preserves the gap and3.1 bounds the line angle by epsilon/(g-epsilon). These are sufficient local estimates, not a characterization of perturbations for which the leading line stays simple or stable. The reverse boundary would infer these hypotheses or this construction from the conclusion, an implication this specific item does not assert."
        }
      ],
      "finite_checks": []
    },
    "thm-sample-centered-covariance-root-n-frobenius": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-identically-distributed-and-iid-random-variables",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(X_i)_{i\\in I}$ be random elements with the same measurable target $(E,\\mathcal E)$. They are **identically distributed** if $\\mathbb P(X_i\\in B)=\\mathbb P(X_j\\in B)$ for all $i,j\\in I$ and $B\\in\\mathcal E$, that is, their laws in [[def-law-or-distribution-of-a-random-element]] agree. They are **independent and identically distributed (IID)** if, in addition, the whole family is independent in [[def-independent-random-elements]]. Independence means mutual independence, not merely pairwise independence. No moment assumption is part of either definition. The empty family satisfies these universal conditions vacuously.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-measurable-functions-preserve-independence",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(X_i)_{i\\in I}$ be an independent family of random elements\n$X_i:(\\Omega,\\mathcal F,\\mathbb P)\\to(S_i,\\Sigma_i)$. For each $i$, let\n$g_i:(S_i,\\Sigma_i)\\to(T_i,\\mathcal T_i)$ be measurable. Then the family\n$(g_i\\circ X_i)_{i\\in I}$ is independent.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-covariance-matrix-of-a-random-vector",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $X=(X_1,\\ldots,X_p)$ be a measurable random vector in $\\mathbb R^p$,\nwhere $p\\ge1$ is finite, and assume $\\mathbb E\\|X\\|_2^2<\\infty$. Define\nthe mean coordinatewise by $\\mu_j=\\mathbb E X_j$ and define the covariance\nmatrix by\n$$\\Sigma:=\\mathbb E[(X-\\mu)(X-\\mu)^T],$$\nwhere each entry is the expectation of the corresponding real random\nvariable. Write $\\operatorname{tr}(\\Sigma)=\\sum_{j=1}^p\\Sigma_{jj}$.\n\nThe well-definedness lemma proves these expectations are finite,\n$\\Sigma$ is symmetric positive semidefinite, and\n$\\operatorname{tr}(\\Sigma)=\\mathbb E\\|X-\\mu\\|_2^2$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "2.2",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-sample-covariance-and-operator-norm-loss",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $n,p\\ge1$ be integers and let $X_1,\\ldots,X_n$ be independent identically distributed random vectors in $\\mathbb R^p$ with finite second moments, mean $\\mu=\\mathbb E X_1$, and covariance matrix\n$$\\Sigma=\\mathbb E[(X_1-\\mu)(X_1-\\mu)^{\\mathsf T}].$$\nThe oracle-centered sample covariance and the sample-centered covariance are respectively\n$$\\widehat\\Sigma_\\mu=\\frac1n\\sum_{i=1}^n(X_i-\\mu)(X_i-\\mu)^{\\mathsf T},\\qquad \\widehat\\Sigma^\\ast=\\frac1n\\sum_{i=1}^n(X_i-\\bar X)(X_i-\\bar X)^{\\mathsf T},\\quad \\bar X=\\frac1n\\sum_{i=1}^nX_i.$$\nFor a matrix estimate $A$, squared operator loss is $\\|A-\\Sigma\\|_{\\mathrm{op}}^2$, with the operator norm of [[def-operator-norm-on-a-finite-dimensional-inner-product-space]]. This definition distinguishes the known-mean raw second moment from the estimator that removes an unknown mean.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "2.2",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "lem-variance-and-covariance-identities-for-random-variables",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $X,Y$ be square-integrable real random variables on one probability space.\nThen\n$$\\operatorname{Var}(X)=\\mathbb E[X^2]-\\mathbb E[X]^2,$$\n$$\\operatorname{Cov}(X,Y)=\\mathbb E[XY]-\\mathbb E[X]\\mathbb E[Y].$$\nMoreover, covariance is symmetric and bilinear on finite linear combinations.\nOn finite full-power-set probability spaces these formulas reduce to the\npublished finite identities.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "cor-covariance-vanishes-under-independence",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "If $X$ and $Y$ are independent square-integrable real random variables, then\n$$\\operatorname{Cov}(X,Y)=0.$$\n\nThus independence implies zero covariance. The converse is false in general.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "cor-expectation-linearity-monotonicity-and-modulus-bound",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $X,Y$ be integrable real or complex random variables on one probability\nspace.\n\n1. For scalars $a,b$,\n   $$\\mathbb E[aX+bY]=a\\,\\mathbb E[X]+b\\,\\mathbb E[Y].$$\n2. If $X$ and $Y$ are real-valued and $X\\le Y$ almost surely, then\n   $$\\mathbb E[X]\\le\\mathbb E[Y].$$\n3. $$|\\mathbb E[X]|\\le\\mathbb E[|X|].$$",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "cor-markov-inequality-for-random-variables",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "If $X:\\Omega\\to[0,+\\infty]$ is a nonnegative random variable on a probability\nspace and $a>0$, then\n$$\\mathbb P(X\\ge a)\\le\\frac{\\mathbb E[X]}{a}.$$",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "def-op-and-little-op-stochastic-bounds",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For each positive integer $n$, let $(\\Omega_n,\\mathcal A_n,P_n)$ be a probability space and let $X_n:\\Omega_n\\to\\mathbb R^d$ be measurable into the Borel sigma-algebra generated by the Euclidean open sets, where $d\\ge1$ is fixed and $\\mathbb R^d$ has the Euclidean norm $\\|\\cdot\\|_2$ ([[def-euclidean-spheres-and-closed-balls]], [[def-random-element-and-real-random-variable]], [[def-probability-measure]]). The spaces and measures may depend on $n$; no common probability space is required.\n\nWrite $X_n=o_P(1)$ if for every $\\varepsilon>0$,\n$$P_n(\\|X_n\\|_2>\\varepsilon)\\longrightarrow0.$$\nWrite $X_n=O_P(1)$, or say that $(X_n)$ is bounded in probability, if for every $\\eta>0$ there is $M>0$ such that\n$$\\limsup_{n\\to\\infty}P_n(\\|X_n\\|_2>M)\\le\\eta.$$\nFor real-valued variables on one common probability space, $o_P(1)$ is convergence in probability to zero as defined in [[def-convergence-in-probability]].\n\nThe norms and tail probabilities are part of the definition. Independence is not implicit in either notation.",
          "uses": [
            "2.2",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F8",
          "source": "thm-cauchy-schwarz-and-the-euclidean-norm",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$ and let $x, y \\in \\mathbb{R}^{n}$, with the Euclidean\ninner product and the Euclidean norm as in [[def-euclidean-inner-product]]. Then:\n\n1. **Cauchy-Schwarz.**\n   $$\\lvert\\langle x,y\\rangle\\rvert \\;\\le\\; \\lVert x\\rVert_2\\,\\lVert y\\rVert_2 ,$$\n   with equality if and only if there is a pair $(\\lambda,\\mu) \\ne (0,0)$ of\n   reals with $\\lambda x_k = \\mu y_k$ for every $k < n$.\n2. **$\\lVert\\cdot\\rVert_2$ is a norm on $\\mathbb{R}^{n}$**\n   ([[def-norm-and-normed-space]]), for **every** $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$; the metric\n   it induces is $d_2$ of [[lem-metrics-on-rn]] whenever $n \\ge 1$.\n3. **Parallelogram law.**\n   $$\\lVert x+y\\rVert_2^{2} + \\lVert x-y\\rVert_2^{2} \\;=\\; 2\\lVert x\\rVert_2^{2} + 2\\lVert y\\rVert_2^{2} .$$\n4. **Polarisation.**\n   $$\\langle x,y\\rangle \\;=\\; \\tfrac14\\Bigl(\\lVert x+y\\rVert_2^{2} - \\lVert x-y\\rVert_2^{2}\\Bigr),$$\n   so the inner product is recovered from the norm it induces.\n\n**Clause 1 is a citation, not a new proof.** The inequality and its equality case\nare the published [[thm-cauchy-schwarz-finite]], stated there for two lists of\nreals; all that happens below is that it is read in the vector notation of\n[[def-euclidean-inner-product]]. Re-proving it here would put two proofs of one\nstatement in the library.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Since $t^2\\le1+t^4$ for $t\\ge0$, the fourth-moment assumption gives $\\mathbb E\\|X\\|_2^2<\\infty$. Then $\\|X\\|_2\\le1+\\|X\\|_2^2$ gives $\\mathbb E\\|X\\|_2<\\infty$, so the coordinatewise mean $\\mu$ is finite. Put $Y_{n,i}=X_{n,i}-\\mu$ and $M_n=n^{-1}\\sum_iY_{n,i}Y_{n,i}^{\\mathsf T}$. By [F8], $\\|Y_{n,1}\\|_2\\le\\|X_{n,1}\\|_2+\\|\\mu\\|_2$. For $a,b\\ge0$, $2ab\\le a^2+b^2$ gives $(a+b)^2\\le2(a^2+b^2)$ and then $(a+b)^4\\le4(a^2+b^2)^2\\le8(a^4+b^4)$. Hence $\\mathbb E\\|Y_{n,1}\\|_2^4\\le8(\\mathbb E\\|X\\|_2^4+\\|\\mu\\|_2^4)<\\infty$. For each $j,k$, the variables $W_{n,i}^{jk}:=Y_{n,i,j}Y_{n,i,k}-\\Sigma_{jk}$ are centered and square-integrable because $(Y_{n,i,j}Y_{n,i,k})^2\\le\\|Y_{n,i}\\|_2^4$. The map $y\\mapsto y_jy_k-\\Sigma_{jk}$ is Borel, so [F1] and [F2] make $(W_{n,i}^{jk})_{i=1}^n$ independent.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F8",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Let $\\bar Y_n=n^{-1}\\sum_iY_{n,i}$. By [F1] and [F2], the coordinate variables $Y_{n,i,j}$ are independent across $i$. Thus [F4] makes their cross-covariances zero, and [F4] and [F5] give $\\mathbb E\\|\\bar Y_n\\|_2^2=\\operatorname{tr}(\\Sigma)/n$. Also $\\|\\bar Y_n\\bar Y_n^{\\mathsf T}\\|_F=\\|\\bar Y_n\\|_2^2$ by expanding both squares. For every $C>0$, [F6] therefore gives $\\mathbb P(\\sqrt n\\|\\bar Y_n\\bar Y_n^{\\mathsf T}\\|_F>C) \\le\\operatorname{tr}(\\Sigma)/(C\\sqrt n)\\to0$. Thus this centered-mean term converges to zero in probability.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "F5",
            "F6",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-2",
          "claim": "For fixed $j,k$, all $W_{n,i}^{jk}$ are the same Borel function of the iid vectors $Y_{n,i}$, so they are iid: their independence follows from [F1] and [F2], and equal laws follow because a measurable map sends equal input laws to equal output laws. Thus [F4] gives $\\mathbb E[(M_{n,jk}-\\Sigma_{jk})^2] =n^{-2}\\sum_i\\operatorname{Var}(W_{n,i}^{jk}) =n^{-1}\\operatorname{Var}(W_{n,1}^{jk})$. Since $\\mathbb E[Y_{n,1,j}Y_{n,1,k}]=\\Sigma_{jk}$, $\\operatorname{Var}(W_{n,1}^{jk}) =\\mathbb E[Y_{n,1,j}^2Y_{n,1,k}^2]-\\Sigma_{jk}^2 \\le\\mathbb E[Y_{n,1,j}^2Y_{n,1,k}^2]$. Summing over $j,k$ and using $\\sum_{j,k}Y_{n,1,j}^2Y_{n,1,k}^2=\\|Y_{n,1}\\|_2^4$ yields $\\mathbb E\\|M_n-\\Sigma\\|_F^2\\le n^{-1}\\mathbb E\\|Y_{n,1}\\|_2^4$. Consequently [F6] gives, for every $C>0$, $\\mathbb P(\\sqrt n\\|M_n-\\Sigma\\|_F>C) \\le\\mathbb E\\|Y_{n,1}\\|_2^4/C^2$, so $\\sqrt n\\|M_n-\\Sigma\\|_F=O_P(1)$ by [F7].",
          "step": "2.2",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "F6",
            "F7",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "Expanding the centered sum in [F3] gives $\\widehat\\Sigma_n^\\ast=M_n-\\bar Y_n\\bar Y_n^{\\mathsf T}$. For a matrix $B$, let $\\operatorname{vec}(B)\\in\\mathbb R^{p^2}$ list its entries; by the definition of the Frobenius norm, $\\|\\operatorname{vec}(B)\\|_2=\\|B\\|_F$. Applying [F8] to these vectors gives $\\|B+C\\|_F\\le\\|B\\|_F+\\|C\\|_F$, including the triangle inequality used below. For any $\\eta>0$, choose $C_1$ so the limsup tail of $\\sqrt n\\|M_n-\\Sigma\\|_F$ above $C_1$ is at most $\\eta/2$ by step 2.2. For all sufficiently large $n$, the probability that $\\sqrt n\\|\\bar Y_n\\bar Y_n^{\\mathsf T}\\|_F>1$ is at most $\\eta/2$ by step 2.1. The displayed Frobenius triangle inequality then bounds the limsup probability that $\\sqrt n\\|\\widehat\\Sigma_n^\\ast-\\Sigma\\|_F>C_1+1$ by $\\eta$. This is $\\sqrt n\\|\\widehat\\Sigma_n^\\ast-\\Sigma\\|_F=O_P(1)$ by [F7], which is the claimed root-n rate.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F3",
            "F7",
            "F8",
            "step 2.2",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
