    "lem-pca-sample-covariance-is-a-measurable-estimator": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-statistical-experiment",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "An **experiment** is a measurable space $(\\mathcal X,\\mathcal A)$, a set $\\Theta$ of\nparameter labels, and a family $(P_\\theta)_{\\theta\\in\\Theta}$ of probability\nmeasures on $(\\mathcal X,\\mathcal A)$ ([[def-measurable-space]],\n[[def-probability-measure]]). The measures $P_\\theta$ are the sampling laws.\nIf data under a parameter value are realized as a random element, its law is\nthe pushforward measure $P_X(B)=P(X^{-1}(B))$ from\n[[def-law-or-distribution-of-a-random-element]]; it is a probability measure by\n[[lem-law-of-a-random-element-is-a-probability-measure]].\n\nThe definition specifies the family of laws only. It supplies no sigma-algebra\non $\\Theta$, no measurability condition on $\\theta\\mapsto P_\\theta$, and no\ncommon underlying probability space on which all observations must be realized.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-parametric-statistical-model",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For an experiment on $(\\mathcal X,\\mathcal A)$, let\n$\\mathcal P(\\mathcal X,\\mathcal A)$ denote the set of all probability measures\non that measurable space. Its **parametric statistical model** is the parameter\nmap\n$$\n\\mathsf P:\\Theta\\to\\mathcal P(\\mathcal X,\\mathcal A),\\qquad \\mathsf P(\\theta)=P_\\theta,\n$$\nregarded together with its parameter set and sample space\n([[def-statistical-experiment]]). The map need not be injective. Unless a\nmeasurable structure on $\\Theta$ is supplied separately, no measurability of\n$\\mathsf P$ is asserted.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-identically-distributed-and-iid-random-variables",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(X_i)_{i\\in I}$ be random elements with the same measurable target $(E,\\mathcal E)$. They are **identically distributed** if $\\mathbb P(X_i\\in B)=\\mathbb P(X_j\\in B)$ for all $i,j\\in I$ and $B\\in\\mathcal E$, that is, their laws in [[def-law-or-distribution-of-a-random-element]] agree. They are **independent and identically distributed (IID)** if, in addition, the whole family is independent in [[def-independent-random-elements]]. Independence means mutual independence, not merely pairwise independence. No moment assumption is part of either definition. The empty family satisfies these universal conditions vacuously.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-covariance-matrix-of-a-random-vector",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $X=(X_1,\\ldots,X_p)$ be a measurable random vector in $\\mathbb R^p$,\nwhere $p\\ge1$ is finite, and assume $\\mathbb E\\|X\\|_2^2<\\infty$. Define\nthe mean coordinatewise by $\\mu_j=\\mathbb E X_j$ and define the covariance\nmatrix by\n$$\\Sigma:=\\mathbb E[(X-\\mu)(X-\\mu)^T],$$\nwhere each entry is the expectation of the corresponding real random\nvariable. Write $\\operatorname{tr}(\\Sigma)=\\sum_{j=1}^p\\Sigma_{jj}$.\n\nThe well-definedness lemma proves these expectations are finite,\n$\\Sigma$ is symmetric positive semidefinite, and\n$\\operatorname{tr}(\\Sigma)=\\mathbb E\\|X-\\mu\\|_2^2$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-finite-second-moment-covariance-matrix-is-well-defined",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "If $X\\in\\mathbb R^p$ is measurable and $\\mathbb E\\|X\\|_2^2<\\infty$, then\n$\\mu=\\mathbb E X$ and $\\Sigma=\\mathbb E[(X-\\mu)(X-\\mu)^T]$ in\n[[def-covariance-matrix-of-a-random-vector]] are well-defined entrywise,\n$\\Sigma$ is symmetric positive semidefinite, and\n$\\operatorname{tr}(\\Sigma)=\\mathbb E\\|X-\\mu\\|_2^2$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "def-estimand-on-a-statistical-model",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For a parametric model with parameter set $\\Theta$, an **estimand** or\n**parameter target** is a specified function\n$$\n\\psi:\\Theta\\to\\mathcal T_0,\n$$\nwhere $\\mathcal T_0$ is a set of target values\n([[def-parametric-statistical-model]]). This definition imposes no measurable\nstructure on $\\Theta$ or $\\mathcal T_0$ and does not require $\\psi$ to determine\nthe full parameter value.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "def-sample-covariance-and-operator-norm-loss",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $n,p\\ge1$ be integers and let $X_1,\\ldots,X_n$ be independent identically distributed random vectors in $\\mathbb R^p$ with finite second moments, mean $\\mu=\\mathbb E X_1$, and covariance matrix\n$$\\Sigma=\\mathbb E[(X_1-\\mu)(X_1-\\mu)^{\\mathsf T}].$$\nThe oracle-centered sample covariance and the sample-centered covariance are respectively\n$$\\widehat\\Sigma_\\mu=\\frac1n\\sum_{i=1}^n(X_i-\\mu)(X_i-\\mu)^{\\mathsf T},\\qquad \\widehat\\Sigma^\\ast=\\frac1n\\sum_{i=1}^n(X_i-\\bar X)(X_i-\\bar X)^{\\mathsf T},\\quad \\bar X=\\frac1n\\sum_{i=1}^nX_i.$$\nFor a matrix estimate $A$, squared operator loss is $\\|A-\\Sigma\\|_{\\mathrm{op}}^2$, with the operator norm of [[def-operator-norm-on-a-finite-dimensional-inner-product-space]]. This definition distinguishes the known-mean raw second moment from the estimator that removes an unknown mean.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "def-statistic-and-estimator",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $\\mathsf E=((\\mathcal X,\\mathcal A),\\Theta,(P_\\theta)_{\\theta\\in\\Theta})$\nbe an experiment ([[def-statistical-experiment]]). A **statistic** with target\nmeasurable space $(\\mathcal Y,\\mathcal B)$ is a measurable map\n$T:(\\mathcal X,\\mathcal A)\\to(\\mathcal Y,\\mathcal B)$, where measurability\nmeans that $T^{-1}(B)\\in\\mathcal A$ for every $B\\in\\mathcal B$\n([[def-measurable-function-between-measurable-spaces]]). If\n$\\psi:\\Theta\\to\\mathcal Y$ is an estimand ([[def-estimand-on-a-statistical-model]])\nand its target set $\\mathcal Y$ is equipped with $\\mathcal B$, a statistic\n$\\widehat\\psi$ with this same target space is an **estimator of $\\psi$**.\nThe value $\\widehat\\psi(x)$ is the estimate at observed data $x$. Giving\n$\\mathcal Y$ a measurable structure is an additional choice; this definition\ndoes not assert that $\\psi$ is measurable as a function of the parameter.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "thm-continuous-preimages-of-borel-sets-are-borel",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "If $f:X\\to Y$ is continuous between topological spaces, then\n$f^{-1}[B]\\in\\mathcal B(X)$ for every $B\\in\\mathcal B(Y)$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Regard $\\mathbb S^p$ as the subspace of $\\mathbb R^{p^2}$ with its Borel sigma-algebra. Each matrix coordinate of $T_n$ is a polynomial in the $np$ real sample coordinates, so $T_n:(\\mathbb R^p)^n\\to\\mathbb R^{p^2}$ is continuous. Its values are symmetric because every outer product is symmetric, hence it is also continuous as a map into the subspace $\\mathbb S^p$. By [F7] it is Borel measurable.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F5",
            "F7",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "By [F1] and [F2], the sample experiment is the stated parameter-indexed iid experiment; [F3] places $\\Sigma_\\theta$ in $\\mathbb S^p$, and [F4] identifies it as the estimand target. The formula in [F5] is exactly $T_n$. Thus [F6] and step 1.1 make $T_n$ a statistic and, since its target space is the same $\\mathbb S^p$, an estimator of $\\psi$.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "F5",
            "F6",
            "step 1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
