    "thm-pca-leading-direction-maximizes-variance": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-covariance-and-correlation-principal-component-analysis",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $\\mathsf E=((\\mathcal X,\\mathcal A),\\Theta,(P_\\theta)_{\\theta\\in\\Theta})$\nbe a parametric statistical model, let $p\\ge1$, and let\n$X:\\mathcal X\\to\\mathbb R^p$ be a measurable random vector with\n$\\mathbb E_\\theta\\|X\\|_2^2<\\infty$ for every $\\theta\\in\\Theta$. Write\n$\\mu_\\theta=\\mathbb E_\\theta X$ and\n$\\Sigma_\\theta=\\operatorname{Cov}_\\theta(X)$ as in\n[[def-covariance-matrix-of-a-random-vector]]. The map\n$\\psi(\\theta)=\\Sigma_\\theta$ is the covariance-matrix estimand on this model\n([[def-estimand-on-a-statistical-model]]).\n\nFor each $\\theta$, list the real eigenvalues of $\\Sigma_\\theta$ in\nnonincreasing order and counting multiplicity. The **covariance-PCA\ndecomposition** is the list of eigenspaces of $\\Sigma_\\theta$, ordered by\ntheir distinct eigenvalues from largest to smallest. Equivalently, for each\ndistinct eigenvalue $\\lambda$, its component subspace is\n$\\ker(\\Sigma_\\theta-\\lambda I)$. The top eigenspace is the leading principal\nsubspace; when its eigenvalue is simple it is a line, and either unit\nrepresentative has the same sign-free direction.\nThe well-definedness result\n[[lem-finite-second-moment-covariance-matrix-is-well-defined]] ensures these\ncovariance matrices are finite, symmetric, and positive semidefinite. The\nreal spectral theorem\n[[cor-real-spectral-theorem-for-self-adjoint-endomorphisms]] ensures their\neigenspaces span $\\mathbb R^p$.\n\nIf every coordinate variance $(\\Sigma_\\theta)_{jj}$ is positive, put\n$D_\\theta=\\operatorname{diag}(\\sqrt{(\\Sigma_\\theta)_{11}},\\ldots,\n\\sqrt{(\\Sigma_\\theta)_{pp}})$ and standardize the centered vector as\n$Z_\\theta=D_\\theta^{-1}(X-\\mu_\\theta)$. Its covariance is the correlation\nmatrix\n$R_\\theta=D_\\theta^{-1}\\Sigma_\\theta D_\\theta^{-1}$. **Correlation-PCA** is\nthe ordered eigenspace decomposition of $R_\\theta$. Thus both population\nconventions center first; correlation-PCA additionally divides each\ncoordinate by its positive population standard deviation.\n\nFor an iid sample $X_1,\\ldots,X_n$, **sample covariance-PCA** uses the\neigenspaces of the sample-centered covariance\n$\\widehat\\Sigma_n^\\ast=n^{-1}\\sum_i(X_i-\\bar X)(X_i-\\bar X)^{\\mathsf T}$\nfrom [[def-sample-covariance-and-operator-norm-loss]], where\n$\\bar X=n^{-1}\\sum_iX_i$. Sample correlation-PCA standardizes by the\npositive sample standard deviations when all sample coordinate variances are\npositive. The divisor convention here is $n$, not $n-1$. The sample\ncovariance matrix is a statistic, and it is an estimator of the covariance\ntarget in its sample experiment, as proved in\n[[lem-pca-sample-covariance-is-a-measurable-estimator]].\n\nThis definition specifies mathematical matrices under an assumed law. The\nscientific model contract distinguishes that assumption from evidence that an\nempirical law is adequate; PCA itself makes no such adequacy claim\n([[def-scientific-statistical-model-contract]]).",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-covariance-matrix-of-a-random-vector",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $X=(X_1,\\ldots,X_p)$ be a measurable random vector in $\\mathbb R^p$,\nwhere $p\\ge1$ is finite, and assume $\\mathbb E\\|X\\|_2^2<\\infty$. Define\nthe mean coordinatewise by $\\mu_j=\\mathbb E X_j$ and define the covariance\nmatrix by\n$$\\Sigma:=\\mathbb E[(X-\\mu)(X-\\mu)^T],$$\nwhere each entry is the expectation of the corresponding real random\nvariable. Write $\\operatorname{tr}(\\Sigma)=\\sum_{j=1}^p\\Sigma_{jj}$.\n\nThe well-definedness lemma proves these expectations are finite,\n$\\Sigma$ is symmetric positive semidefinite, and\n$\\operatorname{tr}(\\Sigma)=\\mathbb E\\|X-\\mu\\|_2^2$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-moments-variance-and-covariance",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $X$ be a real random variable on a probability space.\n\n- For $p>0$, the **$p$th absolute moment** of $X$ exists when $|X|^p$ is\n  integrable, and is then\n  $$\\mathbb E[|X|^p].$$\n- If $X$ is integrable, its **mean** is $\\mathbb E[X]$.\n- If $X$ is square-integrable, its **variance** is\n  $$\\operatorname{Var}(X):=\\mathbb E[(X-\\mathbb E[X])^2].$$\n\nIf $X$ and $Y$ are square-integrable real random variables on the same\nprobability space, their **covariance** is\n$$\\operatorname{Cov}(X,Y):=\\mathbb E[(X-\\mathbb E[X])(Y-\\mathbb E[Y])].$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "cor-expectation-linearity-monotonicity-and-modulus-bound",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $X,Y$ be integrable real or complex random variables on one probability\nspace.\n\n1. For scalars $a,b$,\n   $$\\mathbb E[aX+bY]=a\\,\\mathbb E[X]+b\\,\\mathbb E[Y].$$\n2. If $X$ and $Y$ are real-valued and $X\\le Y$ almost surely, then\n   $$\\mathbb E[X]\\le\\mathbb E[Y].$$\n3. $$|\\mathbb E[X]|\\le\\mathbb E[|X|].$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "cor-real-spectral-theorem-for-self-adjoint-endomorphisms",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $V$ be a finite-dimensional real inner product space and let $T:V\\to V$ be\nself-adjoint. Then $V$ has an orthonormal basis consisting of eigenvectors of\n$T$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-courant-fischer-min-max-principle",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $T:V\\to V$ be self-adjoint on an $n$-dimensional real inner product space,\nand list its eigenvalues in weakly decreasing order:\n\n$$\\lambda_1\\ge\\lambda_2\\ge\\cdots\\ge\\lambda_n.$$\n\nThen for every $1\\le k\\le n$,\n\n$$\\lambda_k=\\min_{\\dim W=n-k+1}\\ \\max_{0\\ne v\\in W} R_T(v)=\\max_{\\dim U=k}\\ \\min_{0\\ne v\\in U} R_T(v).$$",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Put $Y=X-\\mu_\\theta$. Finite second moments make every coordinate in the finite sums below integrable. Expanding the centered scalar square and using [F4] gives $\\operatorname{Var}_\\theta(a^{\\mathsf T}X) =\\mathbb E_\\theta[(a^{\\mathsf T}Y)^2] =\\sum_{j,k}a_ja_k\\mathbb E_\\theta[Y_jY_k] =a^{\\mathsf T}\\Sigma_\\theta a$ by [F2] and [F3].",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "By [F5], write $a=\\sum_{j=1}^pc_je_j$ in an orthonormal eigenbasis of $\\Sigma_\\theta$ with eigenvalues $\\lambda_1\\ge\\cdots\\ge\\lambda_p$ and $\\sum_jc_j^2=1$. Then $a^{\\mathsf T}\\Sigma_\\theta a=\\sum_j\\lambda_jc_j^2\\le\\lambda_1\\sum_jc_j^2 =\\lambda_1$. The difference is $\\lambda_1-a^{\\mathsf T}\\Sigma_\\theta a =\\sum_j(\\lambda_1-\\lambda_j)c_j^2$, a sum of nonnegative terms, so equality holds exactly when $c_j=0$ for every $\\lambda_j<\\lambda_1$. This is exactly the unit sphere in the leading eigenspace. Step 1.1 identifies this Rayleigh quotient with projected variance, proving the covariance-PCA assertion.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F5",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "If all coordinate variances are positive, [F1] defines $Z_\\theta=D_\\theta^{-1}(X-\\mu_\\theta)$ with covariance $R_\\theta=D_\\theta^{-1}\\Sigma_\\theta D_\\theta^{-1}$. The hypotheses of steps 1.1--2.1 hold with $Z_\\theta$ and $R_\\theta$ in place of $X$ and $\\Sigma_\\theta$, so the leading correlation-PCA eigenspace maximizes the variance of unit linear scores of $Z_\\theta$.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The definition assumes p>=1."
        },
        {
          "case": "zero",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "If Sigma=0, step 2.1 equality characterization gives every unit direction the maximum zero."
        },
        {
          "case": "one",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "The statement and step 2.1 include p=1."
        },
