          "reason": "The hypothesis is the exact spectral tie lambda1=lambda2 on a finite matrix. Proof1.1 gives eigenspace dimension at least two and2.1 excludes a distinguished vector; no interval of eigenvalues or endpoint limit is part of this claim."
        },
        {
          "case": "nonempty-choice",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "The statement and step 2.1 refer to the canonical kernel and make no arbitrary eigenvector selection."
        },
        {
          "case": "iff-forward",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The repeated-top hypothesis lambda1=lambda2 gives a canonical kernel of dimension at least two in Proof1.1, and2.1 shows no distinguished vector. The statement is this one-way repeated-eigenvalue consequence, not an iff characterization of every possible orientation or PCA tie. The forward boundary would require a separate asserted biconditional, which this specific claim does not contain."
        },
        {
          "case": "iff-reverse",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The repeated-top hypothesis lambda1=lambda2 gives a canonical kernel of dimension at least two in Proof1.1, and2.1 shows no distinguished vector. The statement is this one-way repeated-eigenvalue consequence, not an iff characterization of every possible orientation or PCA tie. The reverse boundary would infer these hypotheses or this construction from the conclusion, an implication this specific item does not assert."
        }
      ],
      "finite_checks": []
    },
    "cor-pca-sample-leading-eigenspace-root-n-consistency": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "thm-sample-centered-covariance-root-n-frobenius",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Fix $p\\ge1$ and a Borel probability law $P$ on $\\mathbb R^p$ with\n$\\mathbb E_P\\|X\\|_2^4<\\infty$, mean $\\mu$, and covariance matrix $\\Sigma$.\nFor each $n\\ge1$, let $X_{n,1},\\ldots,X_{n,n}$ be iid with law $P$; the\nprobability space may depend on $n$. Put\n$$ \\bar X_n:=\\frac1n\\sum_{i=1}^nX_{n,i},\\qquad \\widehat\\Sigma_n^\\ast:=\\frac1n\\sum_{i=1}^n(X_{n,i}-\\bar X_n)(X_{n,i}-\\bar X_n)^{\\mathsf T}, $$\nthe sample-centered covariance with divisor $n$ from\n[[def-sample-covariance-and-operator-norm-loss]]. For a matrix $B$, define\n$\\|B\\|_F:=(\\sum_{j,k=1}^pB_{jk}^2)^{1/2}$. Then\n$$\n\\|\\widehat\\Sigma_n^\\ast-\\Sigma\\|_F=O_P(n^{-1/2}),\n$$\nmeaning $\\sqrt n\\|\\widehat\\Sigma_n^\\ast-\\Sigma\\|_F=O_P(1)$ in the\nbounded-in-probability sense of\n[[def-op-and-little-op-stochastic-bounds]]. No Gaussian assumption is needed.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-local-leading-eigenvector-stability-under-eigengap",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $p\\ge2$, let $A\\in\\mathbb R^{p\\times p}$ be symmetric, and order its\neigenvalues as $\\lambda_1(A)\\ge\\cdots\\ge\\lambda_p(A)$. Suppose its leading\neigenvalue is simple, with gap\n$$\ng:=\\lambda_1(A)-\\lambda_2(A)>0,\n$$\nand let $v$ be a unit leading eigenvector. Let $E\\in\\mathbb R^{p\\times p}$ be\nsymmetric, put $\\epsilon:=\\|E\\|_{\\mathrm{op}}$, and assume\n$0\\le\\epsilon<g/2$. If $\\widetilde v$ is any unit leading eigenvector of\n$A+E$, then the leading eigenvalue of $A+E$ is simple and\n$$ |\\lambda_1(A+E)-\\lambda_1(A)|\\le\\epsilon,\\qquad \\sin\\angle(v,\\widetilde v):=\\sqrt{1-(v^{\\mathsf T}\\widetilde v)^2}\\le\\frac{\\epsilon}{g-\\epsilon}. $$\nThe bound concerns the line spanned by the eigenvector, so it is unchanged by\neither sign choice. It is a local eigengap bound; it does not assert a\ndimension-free Davis--Kahan theorem or a finite-sample inference procedure.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-pca-sample-covariance-is-a-measurable-estimator",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(P_\\theta)_{\\theta\\in\\Theta}$ be a family of Borel probability laws on\n$\\mathbb R^p$ with $\\mathbb E_\\theta\\|X\\|_2^2<\\infty$. For each $n\\ge1$,\nobserve an iid sample $X_1,\\ldots,X_n$ with common law $P_\\theta$ and sample\nlaw $P_\\theta^{\\otimes n}$ on the Borel data space $(\\mathbb R^p)^n$. Put\n$\\Sigma_\\theta=\\operatorname{Cov}_\\theta(X)$ and let\n$\\psi(\\theta)=\\Sigma_\\theta$ be the covariance-matrix estimand with target\nthe Borel space $\\mathbb S^p$ of real symmetric $p\\times p$ matrices. The\nsample-centered covariance map\n$$ T_n(x_1,\\ldots,x_n):=\\frac1n\\sum_{i=1}^n(x_i-\\bar x)(x_i-\\bar x)^{\\mathsf T},\\qquad \\bar x=\\frac1n\\sum_{i=1}^nx_i, $$\nis a statistic with target the Borel space $\\mathbb S^p$, and hence is an\nestimator of $\\psi$ in the sense of [[def-statistic-and-estimator]]. This\nstatement asserts measurability and target type only; it asserts no\nunbiasedness or consistency.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "def-operator-norm-on-a-finite-dimensional-inner-product-space",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $T:V\\to W$ be a linear map between finite-dimensional real or complex inner\nproduct spaces. If $V=0$, define $\\lVert T\\rVert:=0$. If $V\\ne0$, define the\n**operator norm** of $T$ by\n\n$$\\lVert T\\rVert:=\\max\\{\\lVert Tv\\rVert:\\lVert v\\rVert=1\\}.$$\n\nThe next theorem proves that this maximum exists and equals the largest\nsingular value.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "def-euclidean-inner-product",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$. A natural number is a von Neumann natural, that is a set,\nand $n = \\{0, 1, \\dots, n-1\\}$ ([[def-natural-numbers]],\n[[lem-nat-order-is-membership]]), so\n\n$$\\mathbb{R}^{n} \\;=\\; \\{\\, x \\;:\\; x \\text{ is a function } n \\to \\mathbb{R} \\,\\}$$\n\nis the function space of [[def-function-space]] at $F = \\mathbb{R}$ and $X = n$,\na vector space over $\\mathbb{R}$ under the pointwise operations\n([[def-vector-space]]). We write $x_k := x(k)$ for $k < n$, and two elements of\n$\\mathbb{R}^{n}$ are equal exactly when they agree at every $k < n$. This is the\nsame set that [[lem-metrics-on-rn]] calls $\\mathbb{R}^{n}$.\n\nThe **Euclidean inner product** of $x, y \\in \\mathbb{R}^{n}$ is the real number\n\n$$\\langle x, y\\rangle \\;:=\\; \\sum_{k<n} x_k\\,y_k ,$$\n\nthe finite sum of [[def-finite-sum]] applied to the list $k \\mapsto x_k y_k$\n(extended by $0$ beyond $n$, as every finite list in this library is). The\n**Euclidean norm** of $x$ is\n\n$$\\lVert x\\rVert_2 \\;:=\\; \\sqrt{\\langle x, x\\rangle},$$\n\nwhich is defined because $\\langle x,x\\rangle = \\sum_{k<n} x_k^{2} \\ge 0$ (a sum\nof nonnegative terms, [[lem-finite-sum-laws]] clause 4 and\n[[lem-of-square-positive]], the case $x_k = 0$ giving $x_k^2 = 0$ by\n[[def-integer-power]]) and every nonnegative real has a unique nonnegative square\nroot ([[thm-of-square-roots]]).\n\n### Both are defined for every $n$, including $n = 0$\n\nAt $n = 0$ the set $\\mathbb{R}^{0}$ has exactly one element, the empty function,\nand it is the zero vector space ([[lem-standard-basis-of-f-n]] clause 5); the sum\nabove is the empty sum, so $\\langle x,y\\rangle = 0$ and $\\lVert x\\rVert_2 = 0$.\n**This is the first place on this page where the two index regimes diverge, and\nthe divergence is deliberate.** The published metrics $d_1$, $d_2$, $d_\\infty$ of\n[[lem-metrics-on-rn]] are defined only for $n \\ge 1$, because $d_\\infty$ would\notherwise be a maximum over the empty index set; the algebra above needs no such\nrestriction. The boundary in this page runs between the algebra and the metric,\nnot where a reader would guess, and [[rem-rn-conventions-and-scope]] lists\nexactly which items inherit $n \\ge 1$.\n\n### The algebra of the inner product\n\nFor all $x, y, z \\in \\mathbb{R}^{n}$ and $\\lambda \\in \\mathbb{R}$:\n\n1. **Symmetry.** $\\langle x,y\\rangle = \\langle y,x\\rangle$, since $x_ky_k = y_kx_k$\n   termwise.\n2. **Additivity in the first argument.**\n   $\\langle x + y, z\\rangle = \\langle x,z\\rangle + \\langle y,z\\rangle$: the list\n   $k \\mapsto (x_k+y_k)z_k$ is the termwise sum of $k \\mapsto x_kz_k$ and\n   $k \\mapsto y_kz_k$, so [[lem-finite-sum-laws]] clause 1 applies.\n3. **Homogeneity in the first argument.**\n   $\\langle \\lambda x, y\\rangle = \\lambda\\langle x,y\\rangle$, by\n   [[lem-finite-sum-laws]] clause 2.\n4. **Bilinearity.** Clauses 2 and 3 together with symmetry give the same two laws\n   in the second argument.\n5. **Positive definiteness.** $\\langle x,x\\rangle \\ge 0$, and\n   $\\langle x,x\\rangle = 0$ if and only if $x = 0$. Indeed a vanishing sum of\n   nonnegative terms has every term $0$ ([[lem-finite-sum-laws]] clause 4), so\n   $x_k^{2} = 0$ for every $k < n$, and a nonzero real has a positive square\n   ([[lem-of-square-positive]]), whence $x_k = 0$ for every $k < n$ and $x = 0$.\n6. **Agreement with the published Euclidean metric.** For $n \\ge 1$ and\n   $x, y \\in \\mathbb{R}^{n}$,\n   $\\lVert x - y\\rVert_2 = \\sqrt{\\sum_{k<n}(x_k-y_k)^{2}} = d_2(x,y)$, the two\n   sides being the same expression ([[lem-metrics-on-rn]]). In particular\n   $\\lVert x\\rVert_2 = d_2(x, 0)$.\n\nThat $\\lVert\\cdot\\rVert_2$ is a norm in the sense of [[def-norm-and-normed-space]]\nis proved in [[thm-cauchy-schwarz-and-the-euclidean-norm]], where the triangle\ninequality is obtained from the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality; it is not assumed\nhere.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-cauchy-schwarz-in-an-inner-product-space",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For all vectors $x,y$ in a real or complex inner-product space,\n\n$$|\\langle x,y\\rangle|\\le\\|x\\|\\,\\|y\\| ,$$\n\nwith equality if and only if $x$ and $y$ are linearly dependent.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "def-op-and-little-op-stochastic-bounds",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For each positive integer $n$, let $(\\Omega_n,\\mathcal A_n,P_n)$ be a probability space and let $X_n:\\Omega_n\\to\\mathbb R^d$ be measurable into the Borel sigma-algebra generated by the Euclidean open sets, where $d\\ge1$ is fixed and $\\mathbb R^d$ has the Euclidean norm $\\|\\cdot\\|_2$ ([[def-euclidean-spheres-and-closed-balls]], [[def-random-element-and-real-random-variable]], [[def-probability-measure]]). The spaces and measures may depend on $n$; no common probability space is required.\n\nWrite $X_n=o_P(1)$ if for every $\\varepsilon>0$,\n$$P_n(\\|X_n\\|_2>\\varepsilon)\\longrightarrow0.$$\nWrite $X_n=O_P(1)$, or say that $(X_n)$ is bounded in probability, if for every $\\eta>0$ there is $M>0$ such that\n$$\\limsup_{n\\to\\infty}P_n(\\|X_n\\|_2>M)\\le\\eta.$$\nFor real-valued variables on one common probability space, $o_P(1)$ is convergence in probability to zero as defined in [[def-convergence-in-probability]].\n\nThe norms and tail probabilities are part of the definition. Independence is not implicit in either notation.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "For every $p\\times p$ matrix $B$ and unit vector $u$, applying Cauchy--Schwarz to each row gives $\\|Bu\\|_2^2=\\sum_j(\\sum_kB_{jk}u_k)^2 \\le\\sum_{j,k}B_{jk}^2=\\|B\\|_F^2$. Hence [F4] gives $\\|B\\|_{\\mathrm{op}}\\le\\|B\\|_F$. With $\\epsilon_n=\\|\\widehat\\Sigma_n^\\ast-\\Sigma\\|_{\\mathrm{op}}$, [F1] implies $\\epsilon_n=O_P(n^{-1/2})$ by [F6]. To see $\\mathbb P(G_n)\\to1$, fix $\\eta>0$ and choose $C$ so the limsup tail of $\\sqrt n\\|\\widehat\\Sigma_n^\\ast-\\Sigma\\|_F$ above $C$ is at most $\\eta$. For all sufficiently large $n$, $C/\\sqrt n<g/2$, and then $G_n^c\\subseteq\\{\\sqrt n\\|\\widehat\\Sigma_n^\\ast-\\Sigma\\|_F>C\\}$. Thus $\\limsup_n\\mathbb P(G_n^c)\\le\\eta$, so the claimed probability tends to one.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "F5",
            "F6",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "On $G_n$, [F2] says that the sample leading eigenvalue is simple and $Z_n\\le\\epsilon_n/(g-\\epsilon_n)\\le2\\epsilon_n/g$. The sample matrix is measurable by [F3]; its operator-norm distance from $\\Sigma$ is therefore measurable. The leading line varies continuously on the set of matrices with a simple top eigenvalue: applying [F2] with any such matrix as the base and a perturbation tending to zero makes the sign-free line angle tend to zero. Hence $Z_n$, extended by zero off the Borel event $G_n$, is measurable. Pointwise, $\\sqrt n Z_n\\le(2/g)\\sqrt n\\epsilon_n$, so [F1] and [F6] imply $\\sqrt nZ_n=O_P(1)$ and therefore $Z_n=O_P(n^{-1/2})$.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F6",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "Fix $\\delta>0$ and $\\eta>0$. Since [F6] defines $\\sqrt nZ_n=O_P(1)$, choose $C$ so its limsup tail above $C$ is at most $\\eta$. For all sufficiently large $n$, $C/\\sqrt n<\\delta$, and then $\\{Z_n>\\delta\\}\\subseteq\\{\\sqrt nZ_n>C\\}$. Hence $\\limsup_n\\mathbb P(Z_n>\\delta)\\le\\eta$. Since $\\eta$ was arbitrary, $Z_n\\to0$ in probability.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F6",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
