    "ex-pca-tied-eigenvalues-and-sign-ambiguity": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-covariance-and-correlation-principal-component-analysis",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $\\mathsf E=((\\mathcal X,\\mathcal A),\\Theta,(P_\\theta)_{\\theta\\in\\Theta})$\nbe a parametric statistical model, let $p\\ge1$, and let\n$X:\\mathcal X\\to\\mathbb R^p$ be a measurable random vector with\n$\\mathbb E_\\theta\\|X\\|_2^2<\\infty$ for every $\\theta\\in\\Theta$. Write\n$\\mu_\\theta=\\mathbb E_\\theta X$ and\n$\\Sigma_\\theta=\\operatorname{Cov}_\\theta(X)$ as in\n[[def-covariance-matrix-of-a-random-vector]]. The map\n$\\psi(\\theta)=\\Sigma_\\theta$ is the covariance-matrix estimand on this model\n([[def-estimand-on-a-statistical-model]]).\n\nFor each $\\theta$, list the real eigenvalues of $\\Sigma_\\theta$ in\nnonincreasing order and counting multiplicity. The **covariance-PCA\ndecomposition** is the list of eigenspaces of $\\Sigma_\\theta$, ordered by\ntheir distinct eigenvalues from largest to smallest. Equivalently, for each\ndistinct eigenvalue $\\lambda$, its component subspace is\n$\\ker(\\Sigma_\\theta-\\lambda I)$. The top eigenspace is the leading principal\nsubspace; when its eigenvalue is simple it is a line, and either unit\nrepresentative has the same sign-free direction.\nThe well-definedness result\n[[lem-finite-second-moment-covariance-matrix-is-well-defined]] ensures these\ncovariance matrices are finite, symmetric, and positive semidefinite. The\nreal spectral theorem\n[[cor-real-spectral-theorem-for-self-adjoint-endomorphisms]] ensures their\neigenspaces span $\\mathbb R^p$.\n\nIf every coordinate variance $(\\Sigma_\\theta)_{jj}$ is positive, put\n$D_\\theta=\\operatorname{diag}(\\sqrt{(\\Sigma_\\theta)_{11}},\\ldots,\n\\sqrt{(\\Sigma_\\theta)_{pp}})$ and standardize the centered vector as\n$Z_\\theta=D_\\theta^{-1}(X-\\mu_\\theta)$. Its covariance is the correlation\nmatrix\n$R_\\theta=D_\\theta^{-1}\\Sigma_\\theta D_\\theta^{-1}$. **Correlation-PCA** is\nthe ordered eigenspace decomposition of $R_\\theta$. Thus both population\nconventions center first; correlation-PCA additionally divides each\ncoordinate by its positive population standard deviation.\n\nFor an iid sample $X_1,\\ldots,X_n$, **sample covariance-PCA** uses the\neigenspaces of the sample-centered covariance\n$\\widehat\\Sigma_n^\\ast=n^{-1}\\sum_i(X_i-\\bar X)(X_i-\\bar X)^{\\mathsf T}$\nfrom [[def-sample-covariance-and-operator-norm-loss]], where\n$\\bar X=n^{-1}\\sum_iX_i$. Sample correlation-PCA standardizes by the\npositive sample standard deviations when all sample coordinate variances are\npositive. The divisor convention here is $n$, not $n-1$. The sample\ncovariance matrix is a statistic, and it is an estimator of the covariance\ntarget in its sample experiment, as proved in\n[[lem-pca-sample-covariance-is-a-measurable-estimator]].\n\nThis definition specifies mathematical matrices under an assumed law. The\nscientific model contract distinguishes that assumption from evidence that an\nempirical law is adequate; PCA itself makes no such adequacy claim\n([[def-scientific-statistical-model-contract]]).",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-repeated-leading-eigenvalue-identifies-subspace",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $\\Sigma$ be a covariance matrix in the PCA definition of\n[[def-covariance-and-correlation-principal-component-analysis]], with ordered\neigenvalues $\\lambda_1\\ge\\lambda_2\\ge\\cdots$. If\n$\\lambda_1=\\lambda_2$, then the leading eigenspace\n$E_1=\\ker(\\Sigma-\\lambda_1 I)$ is a uniquely specified subspace of dimension\nat least two. Every unit vector in $E_1$ is a leading principal direction,\nso the covariance matrix determines this subspace but does not single out a\nunit vector in it.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "cor-real-spectral-theorem-for-self-adjoint-endomorphisms",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $V$ be a finite-dimensional real inner product space and let $T:V\\to V$ be\nself-adjoint. Then $V$ has an orthonormal basis consisting of eigenvectors of\n$T$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-pca-leading-direction-maximizes-variance",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Fix a parameter $\\theta$ in the model of\n[[def-covariance-and-correlation-principal-component-analysis]], and let\n$X$ have finite second moment and covariance matrix $\\Sigma_\\theta$. For\nevery unit vector $a\\in\\mathbb R^p$,\n$$ \\operatorname{Var}_\\theta(a^{\\mathsf T}X)=a^{\\mathsf T}\\Sigma_\\theta a. $$\nConsequently, the maximum projected variance is the largest eigenvalue\n$\\lambda_1(\\Sigma_\\theta)$, and its maximizing unit vectors are exactly the\nunit vectors in the leading eigenspace. When all coordinate variances are\npositive, the same assertion applied to\n$D_\\theta^{-1}(X-\\mu_\\theta)$ says that correlation-PCA maximizes the\nvariance of linear scores of the standardized coordinates.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "The eigenvalues of $\\Sigma_1=I_2$ are $1,1$, and $I_2-I_2$ is the zero matrix, so the defined leading eigenspace is $E_1=\\ker(I_2-I_2)=\\mathbb R^2$. For any finite-second-moment random vector $X$ with covariance $\\Sigma_1$ and every unit vector $a\\in\\mathbb R^2$, [F4] gives $\\operatorname{Var}(a^{\\mathsf T}X)=a^{\\mathsf T}I_2a=\\|a\\|_2^2=1$. Thus every unit vector in $E_1$ attains the same projected variance $1$ and maximizes it; each of its lines is a variance-maximizing direction, while the defined leading PCA subspace remains all of $\\mathbb R^2$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F4",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "The eigenvalues of $\\Sigma_2=\\operatorname{diag}(2,1)$ are $2>1$, with leading eigenspace $\\operatorname{span}(e_1)$. Both $e_1$ and $-e_1$ have unit norm and satisfy $\\Sigma_2 e_1=2e_1$ and $\\Sigma_2(-e_1)=2(-e_1)$. They determine the same line, so even a simple leading eigenvalue does not orient its eigenvector.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F3",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "Both examples use p=2."
        },
        {
          "case": "zero",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "The identity covariance example in step 1.1 has no zero eigenvalues."
        },
        {
          "case": "one",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "Both matrices are two-dimensional."
        },
        {
          "case": "degenerate",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Steps 1.1 and 2.1 display the tied and simple regimes."
        },
        {
          "case": "endpoints",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The two fixed matrices are I2 and diag(2,1). Verification1.1-2.1 contrasts a repeated leading eigenvalue with a simple line and two signs; no interval parameter tends toward the tie, so no endpoint assertion occurs."
        },
        {
          "case": "nonempty-choice",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Steps 1.1 and 2.1 explicitly display all eigenspaces and vectors."
        },
        {
          "case": "iff-forward",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "Verification1.1 treats I2 with a two-dimensional leading eigenspace, while2.1 treats diag(2,1) with leading line span(e1) and two signs. This is a contrast between two stated matrices, not an iff model-identification criterion. The forward boundary would require a separate asserted biconditional, which this specific claim does not contain."
        },
        {
          "case": "iff-reverse",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "Verification1.1 treats I2 with a two-dimensional leading eigenspace, while2.1 treats diag(2,1) with leading line span(e1) and two signs. This is a contrast between two stated matrices, not an iff model-identification criterion. The reverse boundary would infer these hypotheses or this construction from the conclusion, an implication this specific item does not assert."
        }
      ],
      "finite_checks": []
    },
    "ex-covariance-and-correlation-pca-differ-after-rescaling": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-covariance-matrix-of-a-random-vector",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $X=(X_1,\\ldots,X_p)$ be a measurable random vector in $\\mathbb R^p$,\nwhere $p\\ge1$ is finite, and assume $\\mathbb E\\|X\\|_2^2<\\infty$. Define\nthe mean coordinatewise by $\\mu_j=\\mathbb E X_j$ and define the covariance\nmatrix by\n$$\\Sigma:=\\mathbb E[(X-\\mu)(X-\\mu)^T],$$\nwhere each entry is the expectation of the corresponding real random\nvariable. Write $\\operatorname{tr}(\\Sigma)=\\sum_{j=1}^p\\Sigma_{jj}$.\n\nThe well-definedness lemma proves these expectations are finite,\n$\\Sigma$ is symmetric positive semidefinite, and\n$\\operatorname{tr}(\\Sigma)=\\mathbb E\\|X-\\mu\\|_2^2$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-covariance-and-correlation-principal-component-analysis",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $\\mathsf E=((\\mathcal X,\\mathcal A),\\Theta,(P_\\theta)_{\\theta\\in\\Theta})$\nbe a parametric statistical model, let $p\\ge1$, and let\n$X:\\mathcal X\\to\\mathbb R^p$ be a measurable random vector with\n$\\mathbb E_\\theta\\|X\\|_2^2<\\infty$ for every $\\theta\\in\\Theta$. Write\n$\\mu_\\theta=\\mathbb E_\\theta X$ and\n$\\Sigma_\\theta=\\operatorname{Cov}_\\theta(X)$ as in\n[[def-covariance-matrix-of-a-random-vector]]. The map\n$\\psi(\\theta)=\\Sigma_\\theta$ is the covariance-matrix estimand on this model\n([[def-estimand-on-a-statistical-model]]).\n\nFor each $\\theta$, list the real eigenvalues of $\\Sigma_\\theta$ in\nnonincreasing order and counting multiplicity. The **covariance-PCA\ndecomposition** is the list of eigenspaces of $\\Sigma_\\theta$, ordered by\ntheir distinct eigenvalues from largest to smallest. Equivalently, for each\ndistinct eigenvalue $\\lambda$, its component subspace is\n$\\ker(\\Sigma_\\theta-\\lambda I)$. The top eigenspace is the leading principal\nsubspace; when its eigenvalue is simple it is a line, and either unit\nrepresentative has the same sign-free direction.\nThe well-definedness result\n[[lem-finite-second-moment-covariance-matrix-is-well-defined]] ensures these\ncovariance matrices are finite, symmetric, and positive semidefinite. The\nreal spectral theorem\n[[cor-real-spectral-theorem-for-self-adjoint-endomorphisms]] ensures their\neigenspaces span $\\mathbb R^p$.\n\nIf every coordinate variance $(\\Sigma_\\theta)_{jj}$ is positive, put\n$D_\\theta=\\operatorname{diag}(\\sqrt{(\\Sigma_\\theta)_{11}},\\ldots,\n\\sqrt{(\\Sigma_\\theta)_{pp}})$ and standardize the centered vector as\n$Z_\\theta=D_\\theta^{-1}(X-\\mu_\\theta)$. Its covariance is the correlation\nmatrix\n$R_\\theta=D_\\theta^{-1}\\Sigma_\\theta D_\\theta^{-1}$. **Correlation-PCA** is\nthe ordered eigenspace decomposition of $R_\\theta$. Thus both population\nconventions center first; correlation-PCA additionally divides each\ncoordinate by its positive population standard deviation.\n\nFor an iid sample $X_1,\\ldots,X_n$, **sample covariance-PCA** uses the\neigenspaces of the sample-centered covariance\n$\\widehat\\Sigma_n^\\ast=n^{-1}\\sum_i(X_i-\\bar X)(X_i-\\bar X)^{\\mathsf T}$\nfrom [[def-sample-covariance-and-operator-norm-loss]], where\n$\\bar X=n^{-1}\\sum_iX_i$. Sample correlation-PCA standardizes by the\npositive sample standard deviations when all sample coordinate variances are\npositive. The divisor convention here is $n$, not $n-1$. The sample\ncovariance matrix is a statistic, and it is an estimator of the covariance\ntarget in its sample experiment, as proved in\n[[lem-pca-sample-covariance-is-a-measurable-estimator]].\n\nThis definition specifies mathematical matrices under an assumed law. The\nscientific model contract distinguishes that assumption from evidence that an\nempirical law is adequate; PCA itself makes no such adequacy claim\n([[def-scientific-statistical-model-contract]]).",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-pca-leading-direction-maximizes-variance",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Fix a parameter $\\theta$ in the model of\n[[def-covariance-and-correlation-principal-component-analysis]], and let\n$X$ have finite second moment and covariance matrix $\\Sigma_\\theta$. For\nevery unit vector $a\\in\\mathbb R^p$,\n$$ \\operatorname{Var}_\\theta(a^{\\mathsf T}X)=a^{\\mathsf T}\\Sigma_\\theta a. $$\nConsequently, the maximum projected variance is the largest eigenvalue\n$\\lambda_1(\\Sigma_\\theta)$, and its maximizing unit vectors are exactly the\nunit vectors in the leading eigenspace. When all coordinate variances are\npositive, the same assertion applied to\n$D_\\theta^{-1}(X-\\mu_\\theta)$ says that correlation-PCA maximizes the\nvariance of linear scores of the standardized coordinates.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "On this four-point space, $\\mathbb E U=\\mathbb E V=0$, $\\mathbb E U^2=\\mathbb E V^2=1$, and $\\mathbb E[UV]=0$ because two points have $UV=1$ and two have $UV=-1$. Thus $\\mathbb E X=0$, $\\operatorname{Var}(3U)=9$, $\\operatorname{Var}(U+\\sqrt3V)=1+3=4$, and $\\operatorname{Cov}(3U,U+\\sqrt3V)=3$. This gives the stated covariance matrix.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "The positive standard-deviation matrix is $D=\\operatorname{diag}(3,2)$, so [F2] gives $D^{-1}\\operatorname{Cov}(X)D^{-1} =\\begin{pmatrix}1&1/2\\\\1/2&1\\end{pmatrix}$. This matrix sends $(1,1)^{\\mathsf T}$ to $\\tfrac32(1,1)^{\\mathsf T}$ and $(1,-1)^{\\mathsf T}$ to $\\tfrac12(1,-1)^{\\mathsf T}$; hence its leading line is $\\operatorname{span}(1,1)$.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "The covariance matrix has characteristic roots $(13\\pm\\sqrt{61})/2$, so its leading eigenspace is a line. It sends $(1,1)^{\\mathsf T}$ to $(12,7)^{\\mathsf T}$, which is not a scalar multiple of $(1,1)^{\\mathsf T}$. Thus the correlation-PCA leading line is not the covariance-PCA leading line, and [F3] identifies each as the maximizing line for its own projected variance.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F3",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
