    "cex-small-eigengap-allows-fixed-angle-rotation": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-operator-norm-on-a-finite-dimensional-inner-product-space",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $T:V\\to W$ be a linear map between finite-dimensional real or complex inner\nproduct spaces. If $V=0$, define $\\lVert T\\rVert:=0$. If $V\\ne0$, define the\n**operator norm** of $T$ by\n\n$$\\lVert T\\rVert:=\\max\\{\\lVert Tv\\rVert:\\lVert v\\rVert=1\\}.$$\n\nThe next theorem proves that this maximum exists and equals the largest\nsingular value.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-local-leading-eigenvector-stability-under-eigengap",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $p\\ge2$, let $A\\in\\mathbb R^{p\\times p}$ be symmetric, and order its\neigenvalues as $\\lambda_1(A)\\ge\\cdots\\ge\\lambda_p(A)$. Suppose its leading\neigenvalue is simple, with gap\n$$\ng:=\\lambda_1(A)-\\lambda_2(A)>0,\n$$\nand let $v$ be a unit leading eigenvector. Let $E\\in\\mathbb R^{p\\times p}$ be\nsymmetric, put $\\epsilon:=\\|E\\|_{\\mathrm{op}}$, and assume\n$0\\le\\epsilon<g/2$. If $\\widetilde v$ is any unit leading eigenvector of\n$A+E$, then the leading eigenvalue of $A+E$ is simple and\n$$ |\\lambda_1(A+E)-\\lambda_1(A)|\\le\\epsilon,\\qquad \\sin\\angle(v,\\widetilde v):=\\sqrt{1-(v^{\\mathsf T}\\widetilde v)^2}\\le\\frac{\\epsilon}{g-\\epsilon}. $$\nThe bound concerns the line spanned by the eigenvector, so it is unchanged by\neither sign choice. It is a local eigengap bound; it does not assert a\ndimension-free Davis--Kahan theorem or a finite-sample inference procedure.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "The eigenvalues of $A_\\delta$ are $1+\\delta$ and $1$, so its leading line is $\\operatorname{span}(e_1)$ and its gap is $g_\\delta=\\delta$. Also $E_\\delta^{\\mathsf T}E_\\delta=(\\delta/4)^2I_2$, whence [F1] gives $\\|E_\\delta\\|_{\\mathrm{op}}=\\delta/4$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "claim": "The characteristic polynomial of $A_\\delta+E_\\delta=\\begin{pmatrix}1+\\delta&\\delta/4\\\\\\delta/4&1\\end{pmatrix}$ has roots $1+\\delta/2\\pm\\sqrt{(\\delta/2)^2+(\\delta/4)^2} =1+\\delta/2\\pm\\sqrt5\\,\\delta/4$, so the larger root is the leading eigenvalue. If $(x,y)^{\\mathsf T}$ is its leading eigenvector, the first row of the eigenvalue equation gives $y/x=4(\\lambda_1(A_\\delta+E_\\delta)-1-\\delta)/\\delta=\\sqrt5-2$. Thus its line makes angle $\\arctan(\\sqrt5-2)>0$ with $\\operatorname{span}(e_1)$, independently of $\\delta$.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Letting $\\delta\\downarrow0$ makes $\\|E_\\delta\\|_{\\mathrm{op}}\\to0$ by step 1.1 while the angle in step 1.2 stays fixed and positive, contradicting the proposed uniform claim. Here $\\epsilon_\\delta=g_\\delta/4<g_\\delta/2$, so [F2] still applies; its gap-dependent bound is consistent with the example.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 1.2"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The counterexample is two-dimensional."
        },
        {
          "case": "zero",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "For each delta>0 the perturbation is nonzero and tends to zero only in the limit; steps 1.1 and 2.1 establish this."
        },
        {
          "case": "one",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The counterexample requires two eigenvalues."
        },
        {
          "case": "degenerate",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Step 1.1 gives base gap delta>0 at each instance, and step 2.1 takes it to zero across the family."
        },
        {
          "case": "endpoints",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Step 2.1 takes delta down to zero while excluding delta=0 itself."
        },
        {
          "case": "nonempty-choice",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Step 1.2 computes the eigenvalues and eigenvector slope explicitly."
        },
        {
          "case": "iff-forward",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "Matrices A_delta and E_delta have epsilon=delta/4 and angle arctan(sqrt5-2) independent of delta by Refutation1.1-1.2. This refutes a uniform no-gap claim in2.1, without asserting a necessary-and-sufficient criterion for all stable eigenspaces. The forward boundary would require a separate asserted biconditional, which this specific claim does not contain."
        },
        {
          "case": "iff-reverse",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "Matrices A_delta and E_delta have epsilon=delta/4 and angle arctan(sqrt5-2) independent of delta by Refutation1.1-1.2. This refutes a uniform no-gap claim in2.1, without asserting a necessary-and-sufficient criterion for all stable eigenspaces. The reverse boundary would infer these hypotheses or this construction from the conclusion, an implication this specific item does not assert."
        }
      ],
      "finite_checks": []
    }
